problem_statement_tex:
(\textit{USAJMO 2024}) Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer. Rowan and Colin play a game on an $n \times n$ grid of squares, where each square is colored either red or blue. Rowan is allowed to permute the rows of the grid, and Colin is allowed to permute the columns of the grid. A grid coloring is $orderly$ if:



                \begin{itemize}
                    \item no matter how Rowan permutes the rows of the coloring, Colin can then permute the columns to restore the original grid coloring; and
                \end{itemize}



                \begin{itemize}
                    \item no matter how Colin permutes the columns of the coloring, Rowan can then permute the rows to restore the original grid coloring;
                \end{itemize}


                In terms of $n$, how many orderly colorings are there?

